{
  "dom_id": "step-q52200",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the solution to the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2v+3w=10 \\\\ v-\\frac{3}{4}w=5\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $4.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2v+3w=10 \\\\ v-\\frac{3}{4}w=5 & |\\,\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}2v+3w=10 \\\\ 4v-3w=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $3w$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-3w$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $w$-variable, and allows us to solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v+\\cancel{3w} & =10 \\\\ 4v-\\cancel{3w} & =20 \\\\ 6v & =30 \\\\ v & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $v=5$ into the equation $4v-3w=20$ to solve for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4v-3w & =20 \\\\ 4\\cdot 5-3w & =20 \\\\ 20-3w & =20 \\\\ -3w & =0 \\\\ w & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $v=5$ and $w=0.$",
